  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis show the strong upper \nridge over the Southern Plains extending up to northern Ontario with \nsplit flow troughing upstream to the west. The southern trough is \nover the southwest CONUS and the northern one is over the Canadian \nPrairie, supporting the sfc low near Lake Winnipeg at 996 mb. 1030 \nmb high pressure over New England sandwiches the UP in a tightening \npressure gradient today as the low continues lifting north, \nincreasing WAA from south to southwest flow. Mostly cloudy skies \nhave taken over and radar reflectivities suggest spotty showers or \nsprinkles are attempting to graze the western UP. Dry air in place \nmeans little, if any accumulation, will become of this. Patchy dense \nfog has been observed in the east, but opted to hold off on any \nproducts given the increasing insulating cloud cover, transient \nnature of the fog, an strengthening winds. Temps warm from the mid \n40s to mid 50s this morning to the 60s to mid 70s today, warmest \nwest and in downslope flow. Winds continue to increase during this \nperiod as well, reaching 10-15 mph with gusts of 20-30 mph, \nstrongest near the central Lake Superior shores as well as other \nareas of south downslope flow where gusts up to 35 mph are likely \n(50-60% chance). Cloudy and dry weather persists until tonight when \nscattered rain showers return from the west with a cold front. As \nthese showers slowly progress eastward tonight, temps settle into \nthe 40s to low 50s.\n\nShowers linger through Sunday morning over the east, becoming more \nisolated in the afternoon as drier air presses in and the front \ncontinues to the southeast. Rainfall totals will be 0.1 inch or \nless. Westerly winds behind the cold front bring cooler, closer to \nnormal temps peaking in the 50s to low 60s, coldest northwest and \nwarmest in the south-central UP. Better breaks in the clouds in the \nafternoon hours as well as much drier Tds in the 20s in the west \nsupport RH falling into the mid 20 to mid 30s interior west. Higher \nminimum RH in the upper 30s to mid 60s is expected in the Keweenaw \nand east. Thankfully, winds gusts remain under 15 mph where RH is \nlowest with less than a 15% chance of gusts above 20 mph with RH \nbelow 30% anywhere in the UP, limiting fire weather concerns.\n\nHigh pressure passes east over Ontario and the Great lakes Monday \nand Tuesday. Then flow aloft turns more zonal as the strong ridge in \nthe south gives in, allowing for multiple shortwave troughs to track \neast next week into the following weekend. The first wave lifts \nnorth over the Dakotas Monday night through Tuesday, supporting a \nweak low to the west which yields light rain over the west half of \nthe UP Tuesday and Tuesday night. This quickly is followed up by a \nmore vigorous shortwave moving over the same area, sending another \nsfc low to the west of the CWA, keeping shower chances in the \nforecast through Wednesday night. Discrepancies remain on exact \ntrack, but best rain chances (40-70%) are over the west during both \nevents. Guidance quickly begins to struggle after Thursday, but a \nmore active period is anticipated to continue into early November \ncaptured by the CPC's 6-10 precipitation outlook of slightly above \nnormal precip.\n\nOtherwise, temps return near normal in the wake of the cold front on \nSunday peaking in the 40s to low 50s Monday and Tuesday. Lows Sunday \nnight and monday night are expected in the upper 20s to low 40s. \nSoutherly flow returning for Wednesday and Thursday warm temps into \nthe 50s to low 60s with lows in the mid 30s and mid 40s.",
